---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro'

const rustCarrierExample = `use std::collections::HashMap;

enum Side { Plus, Minus }

struct OccurrenceId {
    edge: EdgeId,
    side: Side,
    region: RegionId,
}

struct Occurrence<T> {
    id: OccurrenceId,
    orientation: i8,
    value: T,
    coarse_wall: WallId,
}

type CoarseCarrier<T> = HashMap<WallId, Vec<(i8, T)>>;

fn glue<T>(resolved: Vec<Occurrence<T>>) -> CoarseCarrier<T> {
    let mut coarse = HashMap::new();

    for occurrence in resolved {
        coarse
            .entry(occurrence.coarse_wall)
            .or_insert_with(Vec::new)
            .push((occurrence.orientation, occurrence.value));
    }

    coarse
}`
---
<SiteLayout
  title="Carrier through Rust types | Marici"
  description="A Rust type-system sketch of labelled occurrences and explicit gluing in Marici's carrier."
>
  <article class="section-shell" aria-labelledby="article-title">
    <header class="article-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Technical sketch · Rust types</p>
      <h1 id="article-title">The carrier through the Rust type system.</h1>
      <p>
        The central idea is simple: information that changes which operations are legal belongs in
        the type, not in a comment and not in an anonymous array position.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p><a href="/research/carrier-and-algebra/">← Return to the general explainer</a></p>

      <h2>More than <code>Vec&lt;T&gt;</code> versus a record</h2>
      <p>
        A keyed record is a useful first approximation because it remembers which value is which.
        But <code>HashMap&lt;String, T&gt;</code> is still too weak. A labelled occurrence must
        remember structured provenance: which edge, which side, which region, which orientation,
        and which coarse wall it maps to.
      </p>

      <pre><code>{rustCarrierExample}</code></pre>

      <h2>What the example preserves and forgets</h2>
      <p>
        Before <code>glue</code>, two occurrences may have equal values and the same
        <code>coarse_wall</code> while remaining different typed inputs. They may belong to
        opposite sides of a cut, carry opposite residue orientations, or participate in different
        restriction maps.
      </p>
      <p>
        The explicit <code>glue</code> function forgets the occurrence identifier only at a
        declared boundary in the program. It groups values by coarse wall while retaining their
        orientation. A real implementation could require additional proofs or validation before
        constructing the coarse object.
      </p>

      <h2>Why an anonymous array is dangerous</h2>
      <p>
        An array can preserve multiplicity while losing meaning. If two entries both equal
        <code>y</code>, the array <code>[y, y]</code> cannot tell whether they are opposite sides
        of one edge, two graph locations, two sheets, or accidental duplicates. Once that
        provenance is erased, later code may silently apply the wrong sign or identify objects
        that have different legal maps.
      </p>

      <h2>The mathematical reading</h2>
      <p>
        The two occurrences are not merely two names for one value. They are two source objects or
        incidences mapping to one coarse target. The forgetful map is therefore mathematical data:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`Dₑ₊ ──→ D
Dₑ₋ ──→ D`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Rust's type system is a useful analogy because it makes illegal identifications difficult
        to express. Marici's resolved carrier aims to do the same for geometric and physical
        operations.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
